   "project": "fieldnotes",
   "reply": "I added the `delete INDEX` command. `python tools/check.py` now passes: all 5 tests are green, the line-length rule is clean, and the exit code is 0.\n\n**Red phase.** The four new tests failed before any implementation:\n- Two `store.delete` tests errored with `AttributeError: module 'fieldnotes.store' has no attribute 'delete'`.\n- Two CLI tests failed because the CLI returned exit code 2 (usage error) for `delete`.\n- The existing `test_add_then_load` still passed.\n\n**Green phase.**\n- `fieldnotes/store.py` has a new `delete(index)`. It removes and returns the note at a 1-based index, and raises `IndexError` if the index is out of range. In that case the file is left untouched.\n- `fieldnotes/cli.py` now handles `delete INDEX`:\n  - It prints `deleted: TEXT` and returns 0 on success.\n  - It returns 1 for an out-of-range index.\n  - It returns 2 for a non-integer index.\n  - I also moved the usage string into a shared `USAGE` constant that mentions `delete`.\n- The tests are in `tests/test_store.py`, in the existing `StoreTests` class plus a new `CliDeleteTests` class.\n\n**Things to check:**\n- **1-based index:** I chose this and the error codes myself. `list` still prints no numbers, so users have to count lines to find an index. I left `list` alone to keep this change small. Numbering its output would be a natural follow-up, but it changes the current format.\n- **Check setup:** `tools/check.py` needs `NOTES_HOME` set, so I pointed it at a temporary directory for both runs.",
   "suspended": false
